fcntl_getfd

 SYNOPSIS
  Get a file descriptor's flags

 USAGE
  flags = fcntl_getfd (fd)

 DESCRIPTION
 This function returns the flags associated with the open file
 descriptor `fd', which must be either an `FD_Type' object
 or an integer.  If returns the value of the flags, or -1 and sets the
 value of `errno' if an error occurs.

 EXAMPLE

   flags = fcntl_getfd (fileno(stdout));
   if (flags == -1)
     () = fprintf (stderr, "An error occurred: %s\n", errno_string());


 NOTES
 This function is a wrapper around the OS `fcntl(fd, F_GETFD,...)'
 function.

 SEE ALSO
  fcntl_setfd, fcntl_getfl, fcntl_getpipe_sz, fileno, _fileno

--------------------------------------------------------------

fcntl_setfd

 SYNOPSIS
  Set a file descriptor's flags

 USAGE
  status = fcntl_setfd (flags, fd)

 DESCRIPTION
 This function may be used to set the flags on the open file
 descriptior `fd'.  Here `flags' is an integer and `fd'
 is either an integer or a `FD_Type' object.  It returns 0 if
 successful, or -1 and sets the value of `error' upon failure.

 EXAMPLE

  flags = fcntl_getfd (fd);
  status = fcntl_setfd (flags | FD_CLOEXEC, fd);
  if (status != 0)
     () = fprintf (stderr, "An error occurred: %s\n", errno_string());


 NOTES
 This function is a wrapper around the OS `fcntl(fd,F_SETFD,flags)'
 function.

 SEE ALSO
  fcntl_getfd, fcntl_setfl, fcntl_setpipe_sz, fileno, _fileno

